<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mannheim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mannheim is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>. Founded as a fortress town by Elector Frederick IV of the Palatinate in 1606 and laid out in a grid of lettered blocks, it became the residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">Electors</a> Palatine in 1720. Under Charles Theodore its court orchestra made it a musical centre. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1803 and became an industrial city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mannheim.jpg" alt="Mannheim" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg Buzin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altes_Rathaus_und_Sankt_Sebastianskirche_auf_dem_Mannheimer_Marktplatz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mannheim stands at the confluence of the Upper Rhine and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>, in the Electoral Palatinate region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, on the Upper Rhine Plain. It forms a continuous urban zone with Ludwigshafen am Rhein across the river. The city centre is unusual for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>: its streets are laid out in a grid, earning the nickname Quadratestadt, and most of the blocks are named by a letter and a number rather than by street names. The practice is centuries old and comes from the original use of the centre as a fort, whose internal system was kept when the streets became public.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A brick kiln of 74 CE to the early second century shows Roman settlement. The name was first recorded as Mannenheim in 766, in a twelfth-century copy of a Lorsch Abbey codex, and the place remained a village throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In 1606 Frederick IV, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> began building the fortress of Friedrichsburg and the adjacent city centre with its grid, and on 24 January 1607 he gave Mannheim the status of a city. Tilly's forces mostly levelled it around 1622 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and after rebuilding it was badly damaged by the French army in 1689. The National Theatre, founded in 1779, is called the oldest stage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller">Friedrich Schiller</a>'s Die Räuber premiered there in 1782.</p><p>The city became known for inventions. Karl Drais built the first two-wheeled draisine in 1817, and Karl Benz drove the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a> on the streets of Mannheim in 1886; in August 1888 his wife Bertha Benz made the first road trip by automobile, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pforzheim">Pforzheim</a> and back. The Lanz Bulldog tractor followed in 1921, and Benz and Cie. built the first compact diesel-powered car in 1923. Mannheim became a Großstadt in 1896, and the Schütte-Lanz company, founded in 1909, built 22 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airship">airships</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> air raids destroyed the city centre and killed thousands of civilians, while 2,262 of the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>. The palace and water tower were rebuilt, and the University of Mannheim was established in 1967.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mannheim is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, with a population of over 315,000, and the cultural and economic centre of the Rhine-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> region. It is a major railway junction and river port. Mannheim Palace, the former home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">Prince-elector</a> of the Electoral Palatinate, now houses the University of Mannheim, and the Mannheim Water Tower, completed in 1886, is the civic symbol. The city has been a UNESCO City of Music since 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mannheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Palatinate welcomed Calvinist refugees from France and the Netherlands, who founded settlements such as Frankenthal, and Frederick IV founded <b>Mannheim</b> as a fortified town in 1606.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It has been recorded many times, from Bing Crosby in 1949 and the Beatles' Christmas Record to versions by <b>Mannheim</b> Steamroller (1984), R.E.M., Loreena McKennitt (1995) and many others, and William Lloyd Webber included it among his Songs without Words.</blockquote><a href="/songs/good-king-wenceslas">Good King Wenceslas</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl Benz made the first public drive of his three-wheeled Patent-Motorwagen on the Ringstrasse in <b>Mannheim</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-3">3 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years' War, the Imperial-Spanish army of Johann Tserclaes, Count of Tilly, took <b>Mannheim</b> from Protestant troops under the Englishman Sir Horace Vere.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-2">2 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Imperial Recess of 1803 he gained the Palatinate's right-bank lands around Heidelberg and <b>Mannheim</b>, parts of the bishoprics of Constance, Basel, Speyer and Strasbourg, and several imperial cities, and was raised to elector.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-baden">Electorate of Baden</a></li><li><blockquote>His long, narrow state stretched from <b>Mannheim</b> to Constance along the Rhine.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-baden">Grand Duchy of Baden</a></li></ul></section>
